<div class="mars-map">
<span style="position:absolute; left:25%; top:18%; transform:translate(-50%,-50%); white-space:nowrap; font-size:1em;">[[🕳️ Expedition->Expedition]](if: $expedition_done is true)[ ✅]</span>
<span style="position:absolute; left:75%; top:18%; transform:translate(-50%,-50%); white-space:nowrap; font-size:1em;">[[🌱 Greenhouse->Greenhouse]](if: $greenhouse_done is true)[ ✅]</span>
<span style="position:absolute; left:25%; top:50%; transform:translate(-50%,-50%); white-space:nowrap; font-size:1em;">[[🏠 Habitat->Habitat]](if: $habitat_done is true)[ ✅]</span>
<span style="position:absolute; left:75%; top:50%; transform:translate(-50%,-50%); white-space:nowrap; font-size:1em;">[[☀️ Solar Panels->Solar Panels]](if: $storm_done is true)[ ✅]</span>
<span style="position:absolute; left:25%; top:82%; transform:translate(-50%,-50%); white-space:nowrap; font-size:1em;">[[🤖 Robot Garage->Rover Garage]](if: $garage_done is true)[ ✅]</span>
<span style="position:absolute; left:75%; top:82%; transform:translate(-50%,-50%); white-space:nowrap; font-size:1em;">[[🚀 Spaceship->Spaceship]](if: $ship_done is true)[ ✅]</span>
</div>

Tap a place on the map to explore Mars!

[[Sources and credits->Credits]]


:: Rover Garage {"position":"2225,700","size":"100,100"}

ROBOT GARAGE

Two machines live here: a long robotic arm and a smaller robot with two hands for delicate jobs. On the space station, the real ones are called Canadarm2 and Dextre.

A robot job isn't just pressing a button. The team has to line up the robot's work with the base's other systems, like communications, electricity, and computers. They plan where the cameras go and what path the arm will follow. They also check which software updates are needed and look for problems ahead of time. Engineers on the support team help at every step.

<small>Source: Canadian Space Agency, "Robotics mission control centre".</small>

[[Check tomorrow's robot job->Rover Garage Mission]]

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]


:: Rover Garage Mission {"position":"2225,900","size":"100,100"}
(if: $garage_done is true)[
The robots are ready for tomorrow.

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]
]
(else:)[
Tomorrow the arm has to move a heavy part to the solar panels. Three jobs need to be done first: coordinate with the base's other systems, plan the cameras and the arm's path, and update the software and look for problems.

There's time and energy to do all three, but it will use a lot of energy.

[[Do all three jobs. The robot is fully ready, but it uses a lot of energy.->Garage Full]]

[[Coordinate the systems and plan the path, and skip the software update. It uses less energy, but there's a chance the robot's software freezes mid-job.->Garage Software]]

[[Plan the path and update the software, and skip the coordination. It uses less energy, but there's a chance the arm's power use knocks out the radio or the lights for a while.->Garage Coordination]]
]


:: SETUP [startup] {"position":"1500,200","size":"100,100"}
(set: $energy to 5)
(set: $food to 5)
(set: $equipment to 5)
(set: $health to 5)
(set: $crew to 5)
(set: $data to 0)


:: START {"position":"1875,350","size":"100,100"}
🚀 JUNIOR ASTRONAUT MISSION: MARS

Welcome, Astronaut!

You are living at a base on Mars. Your job is to keep the crew, the food, the power, and the equipment safe.

⭐ Remember: in space, every choice has a cost and you must own the consequences, 
There is no perfect answer. You decide what to protect, and you live with what happens next.

[[Start my mission->Mars Map]]

[[Sources and credits->Credits]]


:: Solar Panels {"position":"1525,600","size":"100,100"}
SOLAR PANELS

Rows of flat panels face the Sun. They turn sunlight into the power the whole base runs on.

Mars is dusty, and the wind blows red dust onto the panels all the time.

(if: $storm_done is true)[
The panels are quiet now. The storm is over.

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]
]
(else:)[
An alarm goes off at the control box. A solar storm is heading for the base.

[[Check the alert->Storm]]

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]
]


:: Spaceship {"position":"3000,700","size":"100,100"}
SPACESHIP

The ship that brought the crew to Mars. It landed at the edge of the base and hasn't moved since.

Inside there's a cockpit with screens and controls, a small cargo bay, and the seats the crew strapped into during the trip. Most of the equipment is turned off to save power.

Someday it will carry the crew back to Earth.

[[Check the ship's systems->Spaceship Mission]]

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]


:: Spaceship Mission {"position":"3000,825","size":"100,100"}
(if: $ship_done is true)[
The mission is complete.
]
(else-if: $data_sent is true)[
(set: $ship_done to true)
You connect the ship to the base. The results travel to Earth, and the engines wake up.

(if: $data >= 5)[
🏆 MISSION COMPLETE! You sent the full data set and the crew is ready to go home. Scientists on Earth will study every piece of it.
]
(else:)[
⭐ MISSION COMPLETE! You sent part of the data. It is useful, but a full data set would have taught the scientists more.
]

(link: "Play again")[(reload:)]
]
(else-if: $expedition_done is true and $data < 1)[
The ship's computer checks the files and finds nothing to send. The data from the expedition was lost or never collected.

Mission incomplete. Astronauts learn from missions like this one: next time, protect the data.

(link: "Try the mission again")[(reload:)]
]
(else:)[
The ship is ready, but it has nothing to send yet. Collect data at the expedition, analyze it in the lab, and then come back.

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]
]


:: Stay Connected {"position":"1525,1100","size":"100,100"}
(set: $storm_done to true)

(set: $roll to (random: 1, 100))

(if: $roll <= 70)[
(set: $equipment to it - 2)
(set: $equipment_damaged to true)

The storm hits hard. A power surge runs through the base, and some of the equipment burns out.

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]
]
(else:)[
The storm passes without hitting the base. Everything keeps working, and the equipment is fine.

[[Back to map->Mars Map]]
]


:: Storm {"position":"1525,725","size":"100,100"}
(set: $storm_done to true)

STORM ALERT

A solar storm is coming. It could last a few hours or the whole night. Strong bursts from the Sun can damage electronics.

<small>Real science: in September 2017 a solar storm reached Mars. It lit up an aurora 25 times brighter than normal, and radiation on the ground doubled compared to any earlier measurement. Source: NASA / JPL-Caltech.</small>


[[Disconnect everything. The equipment stays safe, but the base goes dark and cold and the food in the freezer may spoil.->Disconnect All]]

[[Stay connected. Lights, heat, food and radio keep working, but the equipment could fail later.->Stay Connected]]

[[Disconnect only the science equipment. Most things stay safe, but tonight's data is lost and some risk remains.->Disconnect Science]]


:: StoryStylesheet [stylesheet]
tw-story {
  background: linear-gradient(#f7f0b8 0%, #f4cf8f 40%, #ea8f5f 100%);
  font-family: "Trebuchet MS", "Segoe UI", Arial, sans-serif;
  font-size: 1.25em;
  color: #3a1a10;
}

.mars-map {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  height: 420px;
  border-radius: 20px;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: url('https://i.pinimg.com/1200x/23/35/b5/2335b5bbd1faf3df2a8eda7902d08a03.jpg') center/cover, linear-gradient(#c1440e, #7a2a08);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1000px rgba(0,0,0,0.15);
}

/* contenedor general de cada zona */
.scene {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 16px;
}

/* bloque 1: imagen */
.scene-image {
  width: 100%;
  height: 260px;
  border-radius: 16px;
  background-color: #7a2a08; /*Cambiar a background-image: url*/
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
}

/* la imagen de cada zona va en su propia clase */
.scene-image-crater {
  /* TODO: replace with a local image, e.g. url('assets/crater.jpg') */
  background-image: linear-gradient(#a8321c, #5a1a08);
}

/* bloque 2: texto */
.scene-text {
  line-height: 1.5;
}

/* bloque 3: botones */
.scene-buttons {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 12px;
}

/* montañas verdes de fondo */
tw-story::before {
  content: "";
  position: fixed;
  left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
  height: 50vh;
  background: #84b8a3;
  clip-path: polygon(0 55%, 10% 35%, 22% 50%, 38% 18%, 52% 42%, 68% 22%, 82% 48%, 100% 30%, 100% 100%, 0 100%);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* montañas rojas al frente */
tw-story::after {
  content: "";
  position: fixed;
  left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
  height: 30vh;
  background: #a8321c;
  clip-path: polygon(0 50%, 15% 20%, 30% 45%, 48% 10%, 62% 40%, 80% 15%, 100% 45%, 100% 100%, 0 100%);
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* tarjeta de texto, para que se lea bien sobre el paisaje */
tw-passage {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  max-width: 800px;
  margin: 20px auto;
  padding: 24px;
  background: rgba(255, 245, 228, 0.9);
  border-radius: 20px;
}

/* barra de medidores (passage con tag header) */
tw-include[type="header"] {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  display: block;
  max-width: 800px;
  margin: 12px auto 0;
  padding: 10px 24px;
  background: rgba(255, 245, 228, 0.9);
  border-radius: 20px;
  font-weight: bold;
}

/* enlaces como botones planos */
tw-link, .enchantment-link {
  display: inline-block;
  background: #e4572e;
  color: #ffffff;
  padding: 8px 16px;
  margin: 6px 0;
  border-radius: 14px;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 0 #9c2f12;
  text-decoration: none;
  font-weight: bold;
}

tw-link:hover, .enchantment-link:hover {
  background: #ff7a4d;
}

/* ocultar flechas de retroceso */
tw-sidebar {
  display: none;
}
